/*
 * EXAMPLE 10 — BinarySearchTree, singular case.
 *
 * 50 is inserted first and becomes the root; 30 then goes to its left and 70 to
 * its right, so the tree has both shapes of child and the run distinguishes a
 * left descent from a right one.  min() must answer 30 — the key that is neither
 * the root nor the last inserted — and delete(30) then removes an interior key
 * with no children of its own.
 *
 * All three keys are distinct because insert's precondition requires it; a fourth
 * insert of 50 would be rejected before it ran, which is the contract working
 * rather than the library failing.
 */
test BinarySearchTreeDryRun {
    sequence: insert -> insert -> insert -> contains -> min -> delete;

    inputs {
        insert[0].key = 50;
        insert[1].key = 30;
        insert[2].key = 70;
        contains[3].key = 30;
        delete[5].key = 30;
    }
}
